  <h1>Types of Pregnancy Discrimination</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Disparate treatment</h2><p>Intentional discrimination based on pregnancy. The classic McDonnell-Douglas burden-shifting framework applies: plaintiff must show a prima facie case; employer articulates a legitimate non-discriminatory reason; plaintiff shows that reason is pretext. Timing evidence (termination shortly after pregnancy announcement) is the most powerful pretext indicator.</p>
  <h2>Failure to provide equal treatment (PDA)</h2><p>The PDA requires employers to treat pregnancy like any other temporary disability for all employment purposes. If an employer provides light duty for injured workers, it must offer the same to pregnant workers with similar restrictions (<em>Young v. UPS</em>, 575 U.S. 206 (2015)).</p>
  <h2>Failure to accommodate (PWFA)</h2><p>The PWFA (2023) goes further: employers must provide reasonable accommodations for pregnancy-related limitations even if the employee is not &ldquo;disabled&rdquo; under the ADA. Interactive process required. Terminating instead of accommodating is a per se PWFA violation.</p>
  <h2>Lactation discrimination</h2><p>Some courts hold that discrimination based on breastfeeding is sex discrimination under Title VII. The PUMP Act (2022) separately requires employers to provide pumping break time and a private space. Retaliation for asserting PUMP Act rights is actionable.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-pregnancy-discrimination-damages-work">how damages work</a> and <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-pregnancy-discrimination">what to do after discrimination</a>.</p>
